# Copy this file to .env.local and it's picked up automatically by
# `next dev` / `next build`. .env.local is gitignored — never commit it.
#
# These 4 Firebase values are safe to share: a Firebase *web* API key is a
# public project identifier, not a secret (Firebase's own docs are explicit
# about this — access is controlled by Firebase Security Rules and the
# Authorized Domains list, not by hiding this key). They're also already
# hardcoded as defaults in src/lib/firebase.js and src/lib/auth-server.js, so
# the app works even without this file — setting them here just makes local
# dev explicit and lets you point at a different Firebase project if needed.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT in this file, and NOT needed for local UI work with `next dev`:
#
#   ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_PASSWORD   — admin dashboard login
#   RESEND_API_KEY, RESEND_FROM_EMAIL — email verification codes
#   IMPORT_TRIGGER_SECRET             — manually triggering the import cron
#
# These are Cloudflare Workers secrets (`wrangler secret put <NAME>` against
# the real deployment), not .env.local values — plain `next dev` has no
# Workers runtime at all, so features needing them just won't work locally,
# which is fine for UI-only work.
#
# For real D1 data (books/authors/etc) with `next dev`, you get nothing —
# it has no bindings. Use `npm run preview` instead (see README's "Run
# locally" section) with sql/catalog_schema.sql + sql/catalog_seed.sql +
# sql/main_schema.sql + sql/main_seed.sql for a small local dataset, or
# `npx opennextjs-cloudflare preview --remote` to bind to the real
# production D1 (read AND write — be careful what you click).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
